WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:05.670
Our goal now is to solve the monkey and
the typewriter problem for

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:09.540
the case of spectral dictionary, and
after we solve this problem,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.360
we will see that it will lead us
surprisingly to Ostrich Hemoglobin.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:21.090
Let's start from finding the probability
that a string Peptide matches

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:26.290
a string started at a given
position in DecoyProteome.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:30.890
And this probability is of course,
probability of Peptide, is

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:36.620
(1/20)^|Peptide|

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:42.780
Now, let's estimate
the expected number of times

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:47.790
Peptide appears in
DecoyProteome of length n.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.769
To compute this number, E(Peptide,n),

00:00:50.769 --> 00:00:55.519
we simply need to
multiply Pr(Peptide) by n.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:01.790
Now, our goal is to find
the expected number of times

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:07.630
peptides from dictionary appear
in DecoyProteome of length n.

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:10.990
And this number,
E(Dictionary,n),

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:16.960
is simply the product of n,
length of DecoyProteome,

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.100
multiplied over the sum through
all peptides in dictionary,

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:27.880
probability of each individual peptide,
and this is simply denoted for

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:33.860
our future references,
n*Pr(Dictionary)

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:40.570
The question that we will be
interested in is: How many

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:45.750
peptides in DecoyUniprot+ are expected

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:50.450
to score at least -19
against DinosaurSpectrum?

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:55.890
I remind you that -19 is the
score of the peptide spectrum match

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.335
between DinosaurPeptide and
DinosaurSpectrum.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:04.450
To solve this problem,
we will first need to solve

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:08.510
the following probability of
spectral dictionary problem.

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:12.850
Find the probability of a spectral
dictionary for a given spectrum and

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:14.880
score threshold.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.970
Input is a spectral vector,
and the score threshold,

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:23.870
and the Output is probability
of the resulting diction.

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:28.500
Before this solves the Probability of
Spectral Dictionary Problem, we will solve

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:33.240
a slightly simpler Size of
Spectral Dictionary Problem, which is:

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.800
Find the size of SpectralDictionary, for
a given spectrum and a score threshold.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:41.970
To compute the size of
a spectral dictionary,

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:46.030
we will introduce the variable size(i,t),

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:52.630
which is the number of peptides
matching i-prefix of SpectralVector,

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:57.380
which is s_1...s_i,
matching it with score t.

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:02.740
We will introduce the variable size_a(i,t),

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:07.980
which is the number of peptides matching
i-prefix with score t and

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:10.810
ending in a specific amino acid a.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:15.279
Well, size(i,t) of course, is

00:03:15.279 --> 00:03:20.770
equal to the sum through all
amino acids a, size_a(i,t).

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:27.100
And since removing the last
amino acid a from a peptide

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:34.620
results in a shorter peptide with
mass i-a and score t-s_i,

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:42.030
we can rewrite this expression for size
as sum through all amino acids a,

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:46.970
size(i-|a|, t-s_i)

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:49.070
And, of course,

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:54.548
we need to add initialization
which will be size(0,0) = 1

00:03:54.548 --> 00:03:57.110
So, we are now

00:03:57.110 --> 00:04:01.620
ready to compute the size of spectral
dictionary using this recurrency.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:06.730
Given spectrum, let's construct
a directed acyclic graph of spectrum

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:09.807
on nodes 0, ..., m,

00:04:09.807 --> 00:04:13.520
with weight(node i) = s_i.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:19.730
So that will be the nodes of our graph and
I've already represented them as simply

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:24.770
weights that I inherited
from the spectral vector.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.170
Let's assume that we have a toy
example of amino acids x and z

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:33.460
with respective masses 4 and 5.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:39.790
It gives us this set of edges for
amino acid x, this set of edges for

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:45.040
amino acid z, and a peptide in

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:52.680
this case becomes simply a path in
the resulting graph from source to sink.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:56.150
And labels of this path of
course spell out a peptide.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:05.410
Now, the score of the peptide spectrum
match is simply the sum of the weights of nodes

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:10.630
on a path in the resulting graph, which
is a score of PSM. In this case,

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:13.770
the score is three,

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:17.910
after there's another pass
corresponding to a different peptide

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:20.320
XZX, the score will be four.

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:26.195
Therefore, to compute the size of spectral

00:05:26.195 --> 00:05:30.896
dictionary, we simply need to construct
a table for computing size(i,t).

00:05:32.235 --> 00:05:37.905
So this is an example of how we compute
values of size(i,t) in a given row-

00:05:37.905 --> 00:05:39.445
I'm sorry, in a given column.

00:05:40.635 --> 00:05:46.690
So in this case,
the recurrency correspond to

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:51.040
value s_i = one.
We continue further,

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:51.856
and in this case,

00:05:51.856 --> 00:05:56.092
the recurrency corresponds to the value
in spectral reactor equal to 0.

00:05:56.092 --> 00:05:58.980
And finally, we fill in all the table.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:05.630
In this case, the final value in the last
element of the spectral vector is 2.

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:11.540
We have just computed the size
of the spectral dictionary,

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:16.230
and it turns out that computing
the probability of the spectral dictionary

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:17.930
is not much different.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:22.636
Once again, consider a spectral vector,
s1, ..., si, ..., sn

00:06:22.636 --> 00:06:27.740
and define Pr(i,t) as the sum of

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:33.600
probabilities of all peptides
matching i-prefix with a score of t.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:39.510
We will also define Pr_a(i,t)
as the sum of probabilities

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:45.690
of all peptides matching
i-prefix with a score of t

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.570
and ending in amino acid a.

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:53.560
Obviously, Pr(i,t) equals
the sum of Pr_a(i,t),

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:59.150
and since removing the last
amino acid a from a peptide

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:04.660
results in a shorter peptide with
mass i-|a|,

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:10.070
score t-s_i, and
20 times larger probability,

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:14.990
we have the recurrence that is
shown at the bottom of this slide.

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:19.490
Please note that the only difference in
the recurrence for size(a,t) and

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:27.100
the recurrence for Pr(a,t)
is this term "divided by 20".

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:33.870
So in the case of computing size(i,t),
that was an example of recurrence,

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:37.430
the only difference for
the case of computing Pr(i,t)

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:43.670
is that we will need to divide
new entries by 20.

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:49.600
And now, we are ready to find
the statistical significance of

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:55.540
the peptide spectrum match between
DinosaurPeptide and DinosaurSpectrum.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:08:00.290
The statistical significance under
the assumption that we search for

00:08:00.290 --> 00:08:04.650
this peptide spectrum range in
the Uniprot database of length

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:08.200
approximately 200 million amino acids.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:13.506
To compute this probability, I will
give you a reminder and a hint.

00:08:13.506 --> 00:08:17.433
A reminder:
PSM(DinosaurPeptide,DinosaurSpectrum)

00:08:17.433 --> 00:08:22.016
has a score of -19.

00:08:22.016 --> 00:08:26.980
And hint:
if you already solved the problem of

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:31.220
computing the size and
probability of the spectral dictionary,

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:38.060
Dictionary of DinosaurSpectrum for
threshold -19 contains an astounding number

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:44.024
of peptides and has probability 0.00018.

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:51.350
Which allows us to answer the question:
How many PSMs with score at least

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:58.840
-19 do we expect to find in a decoy
proteome of the same size as UniProt+?

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:03.440
We are simply solving the monkey and
the typewriter problem, and

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:09.420
in this case we get an amazing number:
over 35,000.

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:16.340
Therefore, finding DinosaurPeptide as
an interpretation of DinosaurSpectrum

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:23.610
is no more surprising than the monkey
typing THE after 200 million attempts.

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:27.720
Actually, there is absolutely
nothing surprising

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:32.350
if it happened in
the monkey typing exercise.

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:36.830
And likewise, there is nothing
surprising in finding DinosaurPeptide

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:43.000
in UniProt+ database as an interpretation
of the DinosaurSpectrum.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:50.650
After it turned out that some peptides
identified in the T-Rex sample

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:55.320
are questionable,
it became clear that all peptides reported

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:59.170
in the T-Rex paper have to be re-analyzed.

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:05.320
And there are 70 T-Rex peptides
reported in the T-Rex paper.

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:09.070
And after receiving criticism
upon his discovery,

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:13.890
John Azara released all
30,000 T-Rex spectra so

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:19.800
that people from all over
the world were able to analyze them.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:24.650
And within a week after the release
of T-Rex spectra,

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:29.490
the Laboratory of Martin McIntosh at
Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:34.270
in Seattle made an astonishing discovery.

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:39.950
They found yet another peptide
in the T-Rex sample that was

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:45.602
much more statistically significant
than any peptide Azara identified.

00:10:45.602 --> 00:10:51.040
The shock was that the peptide
actually comes from Ostrich and

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:52.610
it's not a collagen.

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:54.620
It is a hemoglobin.

00:10:55.720 --> 00:11:01.780
It would be shocking if the hemoglobin
peptide indeed came from T-Rex

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:07.420
because hemoglobins are much
less conserved than collagens.

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:12.460
Furthermore, hemoglobin peptides have
never been found in much younger

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:18.250
fossils, such as the bones
of extinct cave bats.

00:11:18.250 --> 00:11:23.520
These cave bat fossils are so
common in Europe in caves that

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:29.812
they were used as a source of phosphate
to produce gunpowder during WWI.

00:11:29.812 --> 00:11:37.920
Because Azara had analyzed ostrich samples
before analyzing the T-Rex sample,

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:44.930
could it be that the hemoglobin peptide
is a carry-over or the identification of

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:52.320
leftover peptides hiding inside a mass
spectrometer after a previous experiment?

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:57.640
Contamination is a fact of life
in every proteomics laboratory.

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:02.930
Mass spectrometrists are never surprised
when they identify human keratin

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:06.490
in their samples because
the air in any room

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:11.330
contains millions of tiny
human skin particles.

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:14.170
It doesn't matter how many
times you vacuum-cleaned it.

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:18.728
If the hemoglobin peptide is a carry-over,

00:12:18.728 --> 00:12:22.942
then the entire T-Rex sample
has been contaminated,

00:12:22.942 --> 00:12:27.757
implying that all other T-Rex
peptides should be discarded.

00:12:27.757 --> 00:12:33.002
However, Azara maintained that
there was no contamination and

00:12:33.002 --> 00:12:37.970
that the ostrich hemoglobin
must be a T-Rex peptide,

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:42.317
expanding the class of
proteins that can survive for

00:12:42.317 --> 00:12:45.786
millions of years beyond just collagen.

00:12:45.786 --> 00:12:50.892
If the T-Rex fossil is indeed
a treasure trove of ancient proteins,

00:12:50.892 --> 00:12:55.530
then why we should we limit our
search to collagen peptides?

00:12:56.620 --> 00:13:02.420
Why not do a search against all
known from all vertebrates?

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:06.230
Of course, you should use
criteria that are similar to ones

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:10.156
that Azara used, such as allowing for
up to one mutation.

00:13:10.156 --> 00:13:15.746
If we follow this criterion,
then we would find a surprisingly

00:13:15.746 --> 00:13:21.450
diverse array of peptides, including
mouse and human peptides.

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:28.050
Thus, the hemoglobin peptide
didn't help the claim about

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:33.150
finding molecular evidence of
the link between birds and dinosaurs:

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:35.650
it has weakened this claim.

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:39.225
And as the T-Rex peptides
paper continues to age,

00:13:39.225 --> 00:13:43.050
there is no end in sight
to its controversy.

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:46.060
Yet, it was not the first paper

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:50.300
to report the retrieval of
genetic material from a dinosaur.

00:13:51.450 --> 00:13:54.600
In 1994, Scott Woodward announced

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:59.300
that he had sequenced DNA from an 80
million year old dinosaur fossil.

00:14:00.310 --> 00:14:05.960
The most vehement critics of his
finding was, believe it or not,

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:12.720
Mary Schweitzer, who proved that Woodward
had only sequenced contaminated human DNA.

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:19.250
The moral is that, although we often
present scientific discoveries as

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:21.650
clear and noncontroversial,

00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:24.540
the reality is that many of the avenues

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:27.870
of modern science sometimes
fall short of this ideal.

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:31.820
In a sense, the academic battleground

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:36.800
is a part of the appeal of becoming
a scientist in the first place.

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:42.650
But we also cannot help but wonder
if we would have a conclusive answer

00:14:42.650 --> 00:14:47.960
to whether Horner's fossil really
contained dinosaur peptides

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:52.783
if it was shared with
thousands of fossil researchers.

00:14:52.783 --> 00:14:58.285
Fittingly, in her criticism of
Woodward's dinosaur DNA paper,

00:14:58.285 --> 00:15:03.604
Schweitzer wrote: "Real advance in
paleontology will come only when

00:15:03.604 --> 00:15:10.405
it is demonstrated that those studies can
be replicated in independent laboratories."

